<p>In the latest turn, Iran attacked two commercial vessels and reportedly seized two more in the Strait of Hormuz after President Trump said he was extending the U.S.-Iran ceasefire indefinitely while keeping a U.S. naval blockade on Iranian ports in place. The result is a tense stalemate: wider strikes across the region have mostly paused under the truce, but shipping through one of the world’s most important energy corridors remains badly disrupted, and Iran says it will not return to talks while the blockade continues. The dispute is now centered less on direct battlefield exchanges and more on economic pressure and control at sea. Several reports say Iran has started collecting toll revenue from ships in Hormuz even as Washington tries to choke off Iranian exports, and analysts cited across the coverage say the prolonged impasse raises risks for global oil supplies, Gulf economies, and any diplomatic off-ramp. One modestly hopeful sign is that both sides are still operating within a ceasefire framework rather than returning to full-scale combat, leaving room for diplomacy if the blockade dispute can be eased.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/E5acPKsopzoa68bkpfT7QvX49Z4OZE5JxsGastGdapdslTAAn8xenki4lJPlD1xL8XsANz3GWDKn981ruGqsf8JxTjvscc-VvQKuqmC4ELj--PzL_byQJay7uPB0PgMlpygdRAfh2QEBNtji_CBWLKbIam9N13qfbSOOL6-K-Gyoe2OmA7MSaxt5Q6PVn7cno1bMGFIiOOhSwPcV64cy1GH-AusH0WGH8GEcRSVzmklrMV9erEVjWI8KsIBe0qwHAc0Xr6Q' alt='Shipping-linked image tied to reporting on vessels seized in the Strait of Hormuz.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Oil pressure: Oil stayed above $100 a barrel as peace talks faltered, and Semafor said U.S. gasoline prices had climbed to their highest level in almost four years.</li><li>Military buildup: The Guardian said a third U.S. carrier strike group was nearing the Middle East, and a second task force carrying 2,500 U.S. Marines was due by the end of April.</li><li>China role: Al-Monitor said China was working on two tracks, sustaining Iranian oil flows despite the blockade while also deepening ties with Gulf states.</li><li>Iran resilience: Analysts cited by Dawn said Iran’s economy may withstand a naval blockade in the short term, though storage constraints could force some oil production shut-ins within weeks.</li><li>Environmental risk: Satellite imagery cited by Semafor showed oil spills in the Persian Gulf after strikes on energy facilities and tankers, raising risks to marine life and desalination systems.</li></ul><blockquote>If Iran suffers any damage, the damage to the countries in the southern Persian Gulf will definitely be greater, - Saeed Laylaz</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Trump administration: The White House did not describe the strikes on the commercial vessels as a ceasefire violation because the ships were not American or Israeli, according to Mercopress. (<a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/04/23/iran-attacks-ships-in-hormuz-as-trump-extends-ceasefire-without-conditions-and-faces-domestic-backlash'>Mercopress</a>)</li><li>Iranian government: Iran says the U.S. naval blockade is a red line and that negotiations will not resume while it stays in place; separate reports say Tehran has started collecting revenue from Hormuz tolls. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/iran-says-wont-reopen-hormuz-if-us-upholds-naval-blockade/article70896406.ece'>The Hindu</a>, <a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/us-israel-iran-war-amid-us-blockade-iran-says-it-has-banked-first-proceeds-from-hormuz-tolls-11399231#publisher=newsstand'>NDTV</a>)</li><li>Chinese policymakers: Coverage portrays Beijing as trying to preserve access to Iranian oil and Hormuz shipping routes while also expanding diplomacy with Gulf Arab states, reflecting a two-track economic and strategic approach. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/china-gets-around-iran-blockade-gulf-courts-beijing'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Analysts: Multiple analysts cited by PBS and Straits Times say Iran differs from past blockade targets because it can directly disrupt a trade route that carries a large share of global energy exports, giving Tehran more leverage than Cuba or Venezuela had. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/trump-likes-a-naval-blockade-but-iran-presents-major-differences-from-venezuela-and-cuba'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>Ordinary Iranians: DW reported that civilians inside Iran are living with war-related scarcity, exhaustion, and fear in daily life. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/iran-daily-life-shadowed-by-war-scarcity-and-fear/a-76912143'>DW</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/live-updates/iran-war-trump-ceasefire-strait-hormuz-ship-attacked-us-military-buildup/'>Iran attacks ships in Strait of Hormuz after Trump extends ceasefire</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/us-iran-stalemate-running-out-of-time-as-risks-for-trump-and-tehran-grow'>US-Iran stalemate running out of time as risks for Trump and Tehran grow</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/china-gets-around-iran-blockade-gulf-courts-beijing'>China gets around Iran blockade as Gulf courts Beijing</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/trump-likes-a-naval-blockade-but-iran-presents-major-differences-from-venezuela-and-cuba'>Trump likes a naval blockade. But Iran presents major differences from Venezuela and Cuba</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/04/23/iran-attacks-ships-in-hormuz-as-trump-extends-ceasefire-without-conditions-and-faces-domestic-backlash'>Iran attacks ships in Hormuz as Trump extends ceasefire without conditions and faces domestic backlash</a> - mercopress.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/us-israel-iran-war-amid-us-blockade-iran-says-it-has-banked-first-proceeds-from-hormuz-tolls-11399231#publisher=newsstand'>Amid US Blockade, Iran Says It Has Banked 